<%# THE AGREEMENT PANEL — how far your taste and the machine's line up.

    EVERY FIGURE IS BOUND TO ALPINE rather than rendered once, because the operator
    judges twenty tiles in one sitting and a panel that only updated on reload would
    make him lose his place twenty times. The values come from the SERVER's answer to
    each write, never from arithmetic in the browser.

    STACKS TO ONE COLUMN ON A PHONE. Six figures in a row at 375px is six unreadable
    figures, so the grid starts at two and widens to four. %>
<div data-test="calibration-panel"
     class="rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface-alt p-3 sm:p-4 space-y-3">
  <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-2 flex-wrap">
    <p class="label-upper text-muted">Your taste versus the machine&rsquo;s</p>
    <p class="text-2xs text-muted">
      <span x-text="figure('judged')"><%= @tally.judged %></span> of
      <span x-text="figure('total')"><%= @tally.total %></span> judged
    </p>
  </div>

  <%# THE HEADLINE NUMBER, and it reads "not judged yet" rather than zero on a fresh
      look. A page reporting 0 percent agreement because nothing had been judged would
      be a damning result nobody had earned. %>
  <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
    <%# THE SERVER RENDERS THE SAME SENTENCE Alpine would, so the figure is right
        before any JavaScript runs and right for a reader who has none. %>
    <span class="text-3xl font-black text-heading" x-text="rate()"><%= @tally.agreement_rate.nil? ? "not judged yet" : "#{@tally.agreement_rate} percent" %></span>
    <span class="text-xs text-muted">agreement, over what you have judged</span>
  </div>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-2">
    <% [[:agreed_keep, "both keep"], [:agreed_reject, "both drop"],
        [:machine_overpicked, "we over-picked"], [:operator_promoted, "you would promote"]].each do |state, label| %>
      <% chip = AppearancesHelper::CALIBRATION_CHIPS.fetch(state) %>
      <div class="rounded border p-2 <%= chip[:classes] %>" data-test="tally-<%= state %>">
        <p class="text-xl font-black leading-none" x-text="stateCount('<%= state %>')"><%= @tally[state] %></p>
        <p class="text-3xs mt-1 leading-tight"><%= label %></p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# THE SHARPEST SIGNAL, CALLED OUT ON ITS OWN. Three of the four cells describe how
      well the ranker orders the candidates it was already going to rank. A PROMOTION
      says it threw away something it should have kept, which is the only cell that can
      teach it something it does not already believe — so it gets a sentence rather
      than a tile among four. %>
  <%# x-cloak AND x-show, NEVER the `hidden` ATTRIBUTE. Alpine removes `x-cloak` on
      init and then governs visibility through inline display; it does not touch
      `hidden`, so a `hidden` attribute rendered server-side would survive and keep
      this callout invisible at the exact moment a click made it true. %>
  <p x-cloak x-show="tally.promotions > 0"
     data-test="promotion-callout"
     class="rounded border border-primary/40 bg-primary/10 p-2 text-xs text-primary">
    You would promote <strong x-text="figure('promotions')"><%= @tally.promotions %></strong> photo(s) the
    ranking rejected. That is the most useful thing on this page &mdash; it says the
    ranking is discarding something it should keep, which no amount of re-ordering its
    winners would reveal.
  </p>

  <p x-cloak x-show="error" data-test="verdict-error"
     class="rounded border border-danger/40 bg-danger/10 p-2 text-xs text-danger-ink"
     x-text="error"></p>

  <% unless admin? %>
    <p class="text-2xs text-muted">
      Verdicts are recorded by the operator only, so the keep and drop controls are
      hidden here. What has already been judged is shown on each tile.
    </p>
  <% end %>
</div>
